<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I would like to interject my own anecdotal account on these matters, but I will refrain from such. I'm a firm believer that actions make the person, but this doesn't mean it has to permanently define them. While the actions of the two characters are atrocious, they're still human (<em>much like in real life</em>). I don't want to engage in controversy, but I think the main point of this article is that love can be shown to anyone, despite previous failings and horrible mistakes. It's the notion that second chances are still viable (<em>a beautiful theme, which is why I want to see this succeed.</em>) Example: A majority of incarcerated criminals are ashamed of the actions they chose, and wish for a second chance, redemption. Certainly, you can agree that such is possible for these individuals.</p>
