			<div class="content" id="post-content-4228847">
									

<p>A lot of these notes read like &quot;People with legitimate reasons to no longer contact former associates receive fake apologies (or fake forgiveness vice versa)&quot; to the point that I actually thought this was a malicious entity until I read the comments and discovered it was a hamfisted attempt at being heartwarming.</p>
<p>I mean, you've got: a mother who abandoned her child, abusive siblings, a homophobic mother, a wife-beater and an implication that one of the recipients should kill himself. These people need to give a lot better of an apology than &quot;sorry bro&quot;. The only two that are legitimate are the orphan girl and the college dropout. Compound the fact that the apologies aren't actually coming from the people in question but the anomaly, this means they're totally false, implying that the recipients would be mislead to reach out.</p>
<p>The two legitimate notes have other issues &#8212; I don't know too much about the homeless/orphanage system<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-115252-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-115252-1')">1</a></sup> but I'm pretty sure six-year-old girls are not tossed into the general homeless shelter system without a guardian. Usually, they're brought into what CPS-type agency exists in the country &#8212; which would mean back to an orphanage or foster home. The first note really doesn't make a lick of logistical sense.</p>
<p>The college dropout is described as suffering from &quot;stress-related dysphoria&quot;. That's not really an actual diagnosis and not something the Foundation would use: dysphoria is often a symptom of another mental issue. The Foundation would use a more specific term.</p>
<p>But really, I'm downvoting because I legitimately thought the entity was malicious and the fact I thought that means you fucked up your message big time. If that was intentional, I'd upvote.</p>
