			<div class="content" id="post-content-4229753">
									

<p>A few people have already touched on the conflicting intended message and actual consequences of the anomaly and I tend to agree with them. Like many, I've had major issues with a family member. I don't believe forgiveness was ever on the table in my case, even after their death. Blood isn't as strong a bond as its made to be.</p>
<p>Having the anomaly try to maliciously manipulate people would work way better than what I assume was an attempt at delivering a wholesome message.</p>
<p>Alternatively, allowing the grieving party receive messages actually written by deceased others, from some sort of afterlife, would make the article's message seem more genuine. I'd recommend having fewer examples of its effects but developing them more or making them more sympathetic.</p>
<p><strong>edit:</strong> I've changed my rating to +1 following revisions made to the article. Good stuff.</p>

